spake2plus — provenance note
=============================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`frost`/`adaptor`/`oscore` modules — self-contained crypto modules not
folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

spake2plus — clean-room implementation of SPAKE2+ from RFC 9383 ("SPAKE2+,
an Augmented Password-Authenticated Key Exchange (PAKE) Protocol"),
**P-256/SHA-256/HKDF-SHA256/HMAC-SHA256 ciphersuite only** (RFC 9383 §4's
first table row). RFC 9383 is a public IETF specification — not a
copyrightable work (merger doctrine; see CONVENTIONS.md §5) — so no
third-party-source NOTICE entry is strictly required for the spec citation
alone. No reference implementation's source was ported or consulted as a
design reference; it worked from the RFC 9383 specification text
(`https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9383.txt`, fetched 2026-07-12) and the
public API of the P-256 group / `std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256` /
`std.crypto.auth.hmac.sha2.HmacSha256` / `std.crypto.kdf.hkdf.HkdfSha256`
only.

**RFC 9383 vs. RFC 9382 — explicitly NOT confused.** RFC 9382 (a
companion, separately-numbered IETF document, "SPAKE2, a
Password-Authenticated Key Exchange") specifies the BALANCED (symmetric)
variant of this family; this module implements RFC 9383's AUGMENTED
(asymmetric) variant instead, on purpose — the Verifier-side registration
record `L = w1*P` (never `w1` itself) is the entire point of building this
module against 9383. See `root.zig`'s module doc comment for the detailed
distinction; RFC 9383 §4 itself notes that its `M`/`N` point-generation
seeds "are identical to those used in the companion SPAKE2 specification
[RFC9382]" — the RECOMMENDED shared point-generation ALGORITHM is common
to both specs, but the protocol equations, the `w0`/`w1` split, and the
`L` registration record are 9383-specific and are NOT present in 9382 at
all.

The official RFC 9383 Appendix C test vector for the
`P256-SHA256-HKDF-SHA256-HMAC-SHA256` ciphersuite (`kat_vectors.zig`) was
transcribed byte-exact from the RFC 9383 text fetched at the URL above —
this is a public specification artifact (the RFC's own published
conformance vector, the only one RFC 9383 publishes for this ciphersuite),
not copied from any third-party implementation's test suite. Every
multi-line hex field in the RFC's plaintext rendering (line-wrapped at the
IETF's standard ~72-column width) was reassembled by concatenating
continuation lines with no separator, per the RFC's own plaintext
formatting convention — independently double-checked by reconstructing
the published `TT` transcript from its OWN constituent fields (`Context`,
`idProver`, `idVerifier`, `M`/`N` re-derived from RFC 9383 §4's separately
published P-256 table, `shareP`, `shareV`, `Z`, `V`, `w0`) using RFC
9383's own `ComputeTranscript` field order and 8-byte-little-endian
length-prefix format, and confirming the reassembly matches the
transcribed `tt` field byte-for-byte (570 bytes total, this ciphersuite's
vector). This cross-check is ALSO re-run inside the Zig test suite itself,
against the real (not stubbed) `computeTranscript` function —
`kat_test.zig`'s "REAL TODAY" section — so the transcription is verified
twice, independently, by two different programs.

`M`/`N` (RFC 9383 §4's fixed P-256 constants, SEC1-compressed as printed
in the RFC's own ciphersuite table) were likewise transcribed byte-exact
from the same fetched RFC text and cross-checked against the OFFICIAL
vector's `TT` field: the vector's `TT` embeds `M`/`N` in UNCOMPRESSED SEC1
form (a different encoding from the ciphersuite table's compressed
listing — see `root.zig`'s `share_length` doc comment), and the
uncompressed X-coordinate bytes extracted from `TT` at the known offset
match the compressed constants' own X-coordinate bytes exactly — an
independent confirmation that both the compressed constants AND the
`TT` vector were transcribed correctly (a transcription error in either
one would have broken this cross-check).

The P-256 group (field arithmetic, scalar arithmetic, point group law,
`basePoint`, `mul`, `fromSec1`/`toUncompressedSec1`/`toCompressedSec1`)
comes from this repository's own `p256` module, which is itself clean-room
(see `modules/p256/README.md`) and byte-exact to `std.crypto.ecc.P256`;
this module was written against `std.crypto.ecc.P256` and moved onto
`p256` on 2026-07-19 with no source change beyond the import. The
`std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256`, `std.crypto.auth.hmac.sha2.HmacSha256`, and
`std.crypto.kdf.hkdf.HkdfSha256` primitives are used via their public APIs
(MIT, part of Zig itself); no new curve, hash, MAC, or KDF primitive is
implemented here.

BoringSSL (Apache-2.0, The BoringSSL Authors), commit
`922245af6eda11b3f101ab5f542093eb5e7d1a74`, was **built and run once**
outside this repository to capture the frozen `computeW0W1`/`computeL`
cross-implementation vectors in `src/bssl_w0w1_vectors.zig`. Nothing from
it is copied or translated into this repository — only the outputs of
running it were recorded, so no Apache-2.0 condition attaches here and the
root `NOTICE` is unaffected. The full capture procedure (driver source,
build command, date) is documented at the head of that file.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

Consumer note (Matter/Thread)
------------------------------

The `P256-SHA256-HKDF-SHA256-HMAC-SHA256` ciphersuite this module targets
is the one the Matter (formerly "Project CHIP") and Thread smart-home
device-commissioning protocols use for their PASE (Password-Authenticated
Session Establishment) exchange — cited here only as the motivating
consumer for WHY this ciphersuite (of RFC 9383's several) was picked for
this pass; this module does not itself implement, and has no dependency
on, any Matter- or Thread-specific framing, only the underlying RFC 9383
SPAKE2+ primitive.

**Status: complete.** Wire constants/encoders (`m_compressed_sec1`/
`n_compressed_sec1`, `mPoint`/`nPoint`, `computeTranscript`, the `hash`/
`mac`/`kdf` primitive wrappers) and all seven crypto cores (`computeW0W1`,
`computeL`, `proverStart`, `verifierStart`, `deriveKeys`, `proverFinish`,
`verifierFinish`) are implemented, plus `verifierConfirm`, the eighth
function that lets a genuinely blind Verifier emit `confirmV` before it has
received `confirmP`. No `@panic`/TODO stub remains. `kat_vectors.zig` /
`kat_test.zig` validate them byte-exactly against RFC 9383 Appendix C, and
`bssl_w0w1_vectors.zig` / `bssl_w0w1_test.zig` anchor `computeW0W1` and
`computeL` against BoringSSL, the one construction Appendix C cannot reach.
See `SPEC.md` for the per-function specification.
